    <!-- QUESTIONS AND ANSWERS SECTION -->
    <div class="about-card qa-card">
        <h2 class="qa-main-title">📝 C# & ASP.NET Core MVC Questions</h2>
        <p class="bio-text">Course concepts and framework design pattern review.</p>
        
        <hr class="divider" />

        <div class="qa-list">

            <!-- Question 1 -->
            <div class="qa-item">
                <h3 class="qa-question">
                    <span class="q-number">1</span>
                    Is C# a strongly or loosely typed language? Why?
                </h3>
                <p class="qa-answer">
                    C# is a <strong>strongly typed</strong> (and statically typed) language. Every variable, constant, and expression must have a defined type at compile time. The C# compiler enforces strict type safety, verifying valid operations before execution and preventing illegal type conversions (for example, attempting to assign a text string directly into an integer variable will produce a compile-time error).
                </p>
            </div>

            <!-- Question 2 -->
            <div class="qa-item">
                <h3 class="qa-question">
                    <span class="q-number">2</span>
                    Is C# declarative or imperative? Why?
                </h3>
                <p class="qa-answer">
                    C# is primarily an <strong>imperative</strong> language, but it is multi-paradigm and strongly supports <strong>declarative</strong> programming:
                    <br />• <strong>Imperative:</strong> Traditional C# code specifies explicit, step-by-step instructions on <em>how</em> to execute logic using statements like <code>for</code> loops, <code>while</code> loops, and <code>if-else</code> branches.
                    <br />• <strong>Declarative:</strong> Features such as <strong>LINQ</strong>, attributes (e.g., <code>[HttpGet]</code>), and lambda expressions allow developers to state <em>what</em> result is wanted rather than detailing the low-level execution steps.
                </p>
            </div>

            <!-- Question 3 -->
            <div class="qa-item">
                <h3 class="qa-question">
                    <span class="q-number">3</span>
                    Explain anonymous types in C#, why we use them, and give an example.
                </h3>
                <p class="qa-answer">
                    An <strong>anonymous type</strong> provides a quick way to encapsulate a set of read-only properties into a single object without having to explicitly declare a separate class file. The compiler automatically creates an internal class type behind the scenes.
                    <br /><br />
                    <strong>Why we use them:</strong> They are heavily used with LINQ projection queries to shape, transform, or group temporary subsets of data without cluttering the project with one-off DTO classes.
                    <pre><code>// Example:
var student = new { Name = "Emma-Rose", StudentId = "041176907", Active = true };
Console.WriteLine(student.Name);</code></pre>
                </p>
            </div>

            <!-- Question 4 (DYNAMIC FROM CONTROLLER) -->
            <div class="qa-item">
                <h3 class="qa-question">
                    <span class="q-number">4</span>
                    What is the difference between a Value Type and a Reference type?
                    <span class="controller-badge">From Controller</span>
                </h3>
                <p class="qa-answer">
                    @ViewBag.Answer4
                </p>
            </div>

            <!-- Question 5 (DYNAMIC FROM CONTROLLER) -->
            <div class="qa-item">
                <h3 class="qa-question">
                    <span class="q-number">5</span>
                    What is a ‘Property’ in C# as it relates to classes and objects?
                    <span class="controller-badge">From Controller</span>
                </h3>
                <p class="qa-answer">
                    @ViewBag.Answer5
                </p>
            </div>

            <!-- Question 6 -->
            <div class="qa-item">
                <h3 class="qa-question">
                    <span class="q-number">6</span>
                    Describe the MVC design pattern. Explain its components.
                </h3>
                <p class="qa-answer">
                    <strong>MVC (Model-View-Controller)</strong> is an architectural pattern that separates an application into three distinct components:
                    <br />• <strong>Model:</strong> Represents the domain data, state, and business/validation rules.
                    <br />• <strong>View:</strong> The user interface layer (such as Razor <code>.cshtml</code> files) that renders and displays data to the user.
                    <br />• <strong>Controller:</strong> Handles incoming HTTP requests, coordinates business logic with services and the Model, and selects the appropriate View to render as the HTTP response.
                </p>
            </div>

            <!-- Question 7 -->
            <div class="qa-item">
                <h3 class="qa-question">
                    <span class="q-number">7</span>
                    Describe the purpose of the Program.cs class in this project.
                </h3>
                <p class="qa-answer">
                    In ASP.NET Core, <code>Program.cs</code> serves as the application's entry point. It has two essential roles:
                    <br />1. <strong>Service Registration (Dependency Injection):</strong> Configures required services in the IoC container via <code>builder.Services</code> (e.g., MVC controllers, views, database contexts).
                    <br />2. <strong>Middleware Pipeline:</strong> Configures the HTTP request processing pipeline via <code>app.Use...</code> (e.g., routing, static files, authentication) and maps controller routes before starting the web server.
                </p>
            </div>

            <!-- Question 8 -->
            <div class="qa-item">
                <h3 class="qa-question">
                    <span class="q-number">8</span>
                    MVC Core applications can ‘host’ themselves: true or false.
                </h3>
                <p class="qa-answer">
                    <strong>True.</strong> ASP.NET Core applications are self-contained console applications that run their own in-process, cross-platform web server (primarily <strong>Kestrel</strong>). They do not strictly depend on an external web server like IIS or Apache to run and serve HTTP requests.
                </p>
            </div>

            <!-- Question 9 -->
            <div class="qa-item">
                <h3 class="qa-question">
                    <span class="q-number">9</span>
                    Explain what happens in memory when we make a value type nullable.
                </h3>
                <p class="qa-answer">
                    In C#, a nullable value type like <code>int?</code> is syntactic sugar for the generic structure <code>System.Nullable&lt;T&gt;</code>. Because it is a struct, it remains a <strong>value type</strong> on the stack rather than a heap-allocated reference object. In memory, it allocates the underlying value plus an additional <code>bool</code> flag (<code>hasValue</code>). With memory alignment padding, an <code>int?</code> typically takes 8 bytes instead of 4. When assigned <code>null</code>, the <code>hasValue</code> flag is set to <code>false</code>.
                </p>
            </div>

            <!-- Question 10 -->
            <div class="qa-item">
                <h3 class="qa-question">
                    <span class="q-number">10</span>
                    Explain implicit types and why we use them and give an example.
                </h3>
                <p class="qa-answer">
                    <strong>Implicit typing</strong> uses the <code>var</code> keyword to let the compiler determine the variable's type based on the initialization expression on the right-hand side. The variable remains strongly typed at compile time.
                    <br /><br />
                    <strong>Why we use them:</strong> It eliminates redundant type boilerplate (e.g., with long generic collections), makes code cleaner, and is <em>required</em> when working with anonymous types where the type has no explicit name.
                    <pre><code>// Example:
var message = "Welcome to ASP.NET Core!"; // Inferred as string
var numbers = new List&lt;int&gt;();             // Inferred as List&lt;int&gt;</code></pre>
                </p>
            </div>

            <!-- Question 11 -->
            <div class="qa-item">
                <h3 class="qa-question">
                    <span class="q-number">11</span>
                    Explain Git flow and why we use it.
                </h3>
                <p class="qa-answer">
                    <strong>Git Flow</strong> is a branching strategy for Git that organizes collaborative development through standardized branch roles:
                    <br />• <code>main</code>: Stores official production release history.
                    <br />• <code>develop</code>: Serves as an integration branch for newly completed features.
                    <br />• <code>feature/*</code>: Isolated branches for building specific capabilities.
                    <br />• <code>release/*</code>: Branches for preparing, polishing, and testing new releases.
                    <br />• <code>hotfix/*</code>: Urgent patches branched directly from <code>main</code>.
                    <br /><br />
                    <strong>Why we use it:</strong> It prevents broken work-in-progress code from contaminating production, simplifies parallel development across multiple developers, and facilitates clean, predictable software release cycles.
                </p>
            </div>

            <!-- Question 12 -->
            <div class="qa-item">
                <h3 class="qa-question">
                    <span class="q-number">12</span>
                    Explain dependency injection and why we use it?
                </h3>
                <p class="qa-answer">
                    <strong>Dependency Injection (DI)</strong> is a design pattern achieving Inversion of Control (IoC) where a class receives its dependencies from an external container (typically via its constructor) rather than creating them directly with <code>new</code>.
                    <br /><br />
                    <strong>Why we use it:</strong>
                    <br />• <strong>Loose Coupling:</strong> Components depend on interfaces rather than concrete implementations.
                    <br />• <strong>Testability:</strong> Makes automated unit testing straightforward by allowing real services to be replaced with mock objects.
                    <br />• <strong>Maintainability:</strong> Service configurations and lifecycles (Transient, Scoped, Singleton) are managed centrally in <code>Program.cs</code>.
                </p>
            </div>

        </div>
    </div>
</div>

